# Smart Property Analytics

Machine Learning for House Price and Condition Predictions  
Malaki Nyambutora · CS 746 — Perspectives on Data Science

This project uses the Ames Housing dataset to estimate residential sale prices and classify properties into three condition categories. The Jupyter notebook documents the analysis and model evaluation. A local Streamlit dashboard lets users enter property details, generate predictions, and explore the results.

View the project notebook: https://github.com/Nyambutora/CS746-Smart-Property-Analytics/blob/main/ML_House_Price_and_Condition_Predictions.ipynb 
Download the runnable project: https://github.com/Nyambutora/CS746-Smart-Property-Analytics/blob/main/Smart_Property_Analytics_Final.zip

## Project Overview

The dataset contains 2,930 residential sales from Ames, Iowa. I used 2,344 properties for training and model comparison and reserved 586 for held-out testing. I compared four regression models and three classification models using five-fold cross-validation. I selected the price model by mean absolute error (MAE) and the condition classifier by macro F1.

Overall quality and above-ground living area were the two highest-ranked features for sale-price prediction. Remodel year ranked first for condition classification. The notebook reports permutation importance for both models and uses SHAP to explain one held-out price estimate.

## Run the Dashboard

1. Download and extract `Smart_Property_Analytics_Final.zip`.
2. Open a terminal in the extracted folder containing `app.py`. Keep the `models/` folder beside it.
3. Activate the Python environment used to train the models, or install compatible versions of Streamlit, pandas, joblib, scikit-learn, and XGBoost. The saved pipelines should be loaded with the package versions used to create them.
4. Run the following command and open the local URL printed in the terminal.

   
   python -m streamlit run app.py
  

5. On the Predict tab, click Predict with the prefilled values. The dashboard should return $325,413.12 and Low.

The dashboard has four tabs:

   Predict: Edit property details and generate a sale-price estimate and condition category. 
   Model Evaluation: Review held-out metrics, per-category scores, and the confusion matrix. 
   Data and Exploratory Analysis: Compare missing-value counts before and after cleaning and explore living area versus sale price. 
   Model Interpretation:  Compare feature importance for both models and view the SHAP explanation for the prefilled property. 

## Rerun the Notebook

Open Jupyter from the extracted project folder and select `ML_House_Price_and_Condition_Predictions.ipynb`. The `data/` folder contains `AmesHousing.csv` and `AmesHousing_DataDictionary.csv`. Run the notebook cells in order to reproduce the analysis. For a quick review, open the notebook directly on GitHub to see the saved outputs.

## Limitations

The Low, Medium, and High categories are derived from the Ames assessor’s overall condition ratings. Random Forest correctly identified 9 of 34 test properties in the High category, so its predictions should be interpreted with that limitation in mind. The sale-price model was evaluated on historical Ames transactions. Its performance on recent sales or in other markets remains untested.

## Dataset Reference

De Cock, D. (2011). Ames, Iowa: Alternative to the Boston Housing Data as an End of Semester Regression Project. Journal of Statistics Education, 19(3). https://doi.org/10.1080/10691898.2011.11889627




